      '## Long-Term Maintenance and Lifecycle Cost',
      'Brick has the edge on maintenance. Once installed, a brick wall needs essentially nothing beyond occasional mortar repointing every 25 to 50 years. The brick itself does not fade, does not need paint, and does not crack the way stucco does. Over a 50-year ownership period, maintenance costs on brick are minimal.',
      'Stucco requires more attention. In San Antonio, you should budget for sealant replacement every 3 to 5 years, crack repair as needed (typically every few years due to our soil movement and thermal cycling), and a re-coat or repaint every 15 to 25 years. Over 50 years, cumulative stucco maintenance might total $8,000 to $15,000 — meaningful, but still far less than the upfront cost gap between stucco and brick.',
      'When you add installation plus 50 years of maintenance, stucco still comes out $10,000 to $25,000 cheaper than brick for most San Antonio homes. Brick wins on per-year cost of ownership, but only if you plan to stay in the home long enough for the lower maintenance to compound.',
      '## Durability in the San Antonio Climate',
      'Both materials handle San Antonio heat and UV exposure well. Brick is harder and more impact-resistant — hail, landscaping equipment, and kid activities rarely damage it. Stucco is rigid but not as hard, so it can chip or crack on impact, though repairs are straightforward and inexpensive.',
      'The bigger durability question in San Antonio is how each material handles foundation movement. Our expansive clay soils shift seasonally, and both materials respond differently. Brick is flexible at the mortar joints, which absorb minor movement without visible damage. Stucco is rigid and shows cracks when the underlying wall shifts even slightly. This does not mean stucco is failing — it means stucco telegraphs movement that brick hides. The cracks are repairable, but they do require attention.',
      '## Design Flexibility',
      'Stucco wins on design flexibility by a wide margin. It can be applied in smooth, textured, or patterned finishes in virtually any color. It wraps around curves, fills in reveals, and works with every San Antonio architectural style from Spanish Colonial to contemporary modern. Brick comes in a fixed set of colors and sizes, and while the range is broad, it cannot replicate the smooth monolithic surface of a stucco Santa Barbara finish or the intricate detailing possible with EIFS.',
      'Many San Antonio homes combine the two: brick on the lower courses or accent walls, stucco above. This hybrid approach gives you brick durability where it matters most — at ground level where impact and splash-back are most common — and stucco design flexibility on the upper walls. It is one of the most popular exterior combinations in the Stone Oak, Alamo Heights, and Hill Country custom home markets.',
      '## Which Is Better for San Antonio?',
      'There is no single right answer. Brick is the better investment if you have the upfront budget, plan to own the home for 20+ years, and prefer a low-maintenance exterior. Stucco is the better choice if you want to keep upfront costs manageable, value design flexibility, or are building in a Spanish Colonial or Mediterranean style where stucco is the authentic look. For most San Antonio homeowners working within a realistic budget, stucco delivers the best combination of value, climate performance, and curb appeal.',
