    excerpt: 'A detailed comparison of stucco and fiber cement (Hardie Board) siding for San Antonio homes — covering cost, durability, maintenance, and climate performance.',
    category: 'Education',
    date: 'September 2026',
    image: 'https://tsybcnnjylmvhsxzknug.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/sign/San%20Antonio%20Stucco/stucco-installation-san-antonio.jpg?token=eyJraWQiOiJzdG9yYWdlLXVybC1zaWduaW5nLWtleV81N2ZkNDYwMC00NmYxLTQ0YWItYmZiYi1jODY3N2Y3YjM1MzgiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJ1cmwiOiJTYW4gQW50b25pbyBTdHVjY28vc3R1Y2NvLWluc3RhbGxhdGlvbi1zYW4tYW50b25pby5qcGciLCJpYXQiOjE3Nzc1ODA3NTUsImV4cCI6MTgwOTExNjc1NX0.cWcMPhX9vRkqXTlsDNnl5VuUfRpXOXH-xwBmb3M4dOg',
    relatedService: '/stucco-installation',
    content: [
      'When San Antonio homeowners consider exterior cladding options, two materials dominate the conversation: traditional stucco and fiber cement siding, best known by the brand name Hardie Board. Both are durable, fire-resistant, and widely used in Texas, but they differ significantly in cost, lifespan, maintenance, appearance, and how they perform in our specific climate. Here is an honest comparison to help you make the right choice for your home.',
      '## What Is Hardie Board?',
      'Hardie Board is a brand of fiber cement siding manufactured by James Hardie. It is made from a mixture of cement, sand, and cellulose fibers pressed into planks, panels, or shingles. It is engineered to resist rot, fire, and insect damage, and it comes pre-primed or pre-painted in a range of colors. Fiber cement siding has gained significant market share nationally over the past two decades, particularly in regions where traditional wood siding was previously dominant. It is installed as individual overlapping planks or flat panels fastened to the wall sheathing.',
      '## Cost Comparison',
      'Installed costs in the San Antonio market typically range from $8 to $15 per square foot for traditional three-coat stucco and $6 to $12 per square foot for Hardie Board siding. Stucco\'s higher upfront cost reflects its labor-intensive application — three separate coats applied by hand over lath and moisture barriers, with curing time between each coat. Fiber cement is faster to install since the panels are manufactured off-site and nailed into place. However, the upfront price does not tell the full story — long-term maintenance and replacement costs shift the calculation significantly over a 30 to 50 year ownership period.',
      '## Durability and Lifespan',
      'Properly installed stucco has an expected lifespan of 50 to 80 years, and many stucco homes in San Antonio\'s older neighborhoods have original surfaces that have lasted even longer with basic maintenance. Hardie Board carries a manufacturer warranty of 30 years on the substrate (not the paint finish) and typically performs well for 30 to 50 years before replacement becomes necessary. Stucco\'s longevity advantage comes from its monolithic application — it forms a single, continuous shell with no joints or seams where water can penetrate. Fiber cement\'s joints, though caulked and flashed, are potential entry points for moisture over time as caulk ages and shrinks. For more on <a href="/blog/how-long-does-stucco-last-san-antonio">how long stucco lasts</a>, see our detailed breakdown.',
      '## Maintenance Requirements',
      'Stucco requires relatively little ongoing maintenance — periodic cleaning, prompt repair of any cracks, and repainting or re-coating every 10 to 15 years with elastomeric coatings. Hardie Board requires repainting every 7 to 12 years (the factory-applied ColorPlus finish lasts longer than field-painted options), re-caulking of all joints every 5 to 8 years, and replacement of any boards that crack, chip, or suffer impact damage. Over a 50-year period, the cumulative maintenance cost for Hardie Board typically exceeds that of stucco because of the more frequent repainting and caulking cycles.',
      '## Climate Performance in San Antonio',
      'This is where the comparison gets particularly relevant for local homeowners. Stucco\'s thermal mass — its ability to absorb and slowly release heat — provides a meaningful energy efficiency advantage in San Antonio\'s hot climate. A stucco wall heats up slowly during the day and releases that heat gradually in the evening, reducing peak cooling loads. Fiber cement siding has essentially no thermal mass and transmits outdoor temperature changes more directly to the wall sheathing and insulation behind it. On the moisture side, Hardie Board has a slight edge — its individual panel installation allows each piece to expand and contract independently, and moisture that reaches the back side can dry more easily through the joints. Stucco\'s continuous surface relies entirely on its moisture barrier and weep system for drainage, making proper installation absolutely critical.',
      '## Aesthetics and Design Flexibility',
      'Stucco offers unmatched design flexibility. It can be applied to curved walls, arches, columns, and complex architectural details with a seamless appearance. It is available in virtually any color and dozens of textures, from smooth to heavily textured finishes. This is why stucco is the dominant choice for Mediterranean, Spanish Colonial, and contemporary architectural styles that are so popular across San Antonio. Hardie Board provides a clean, linear look that suits Craftsman, farmhouse, and traditional American home styles. It comes in plank, panel, and shingle profiles but cannot replicate the seamless, sculptural qualities of stucco. For a broader look at how <a href="/blog/stucco-vs-other-siding-materials">stucco compares to other siding materials</a>, see our full comparison guide.',
      '## Resale Value in San Antonio',
      'In the San Antonio real estate market, stucco is the dominant exterior finish and carries strong resale value. Buyers expect stucco on most homes, and it is associated with quality construction and the architectural character of the region. Hardie Board is well-accepted and adds value in neighborhoods where plank-style siding is common, but it does not carry the same premium in San Antonio that it might in markets like the Pacific Northwest or Mid-Atlantic where it replaced wood siding. If you are building or re-siding a home in an established San Antonio neighborhood where stucco is the norm, matching the prevailing style protects your investment.',
      '## When Each Is the Better Choice',
      'Stucco is the better choice when you want maximum longevity, lower long-term maintenance costs, design flexibility for curved or complex surfaces, and strong resale value in the San Antonio market. It is ideal for new construction, whole-house re-siding, and any project where the home\'s architecture calls for a seamless, textured finish. Hardie Board is the better choice when budget is the primary constraint, when the home\'s style suits a plank or panel look, when the project involves siding over an existing surface where stucco\'s weight would be a concern, or when the homeowner values a lower-skill installation. Both are excellent materials — the right choice depends on your home, your budget, and your long-term plans. Our <a href="/stucco-installation">stucco installation team</a> can help you evaluate both options for your specific project. See also our comparison of <a href="/blog/stucco-vs-brick-cost-san-antonio">stucco vs brick costs</a> if you are weighing all your options.',
    ],
